index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..1d4cf0bfc32101d3b9daecb58ba74a76abaaaf42
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+# arXiv 2202.13955 — verified permutation-graph cut certificates
+
+> **Build status (2026-09-05): locally verified, external hardening blocked.** All
+> nine local gates pass, but the required four-vendor run could not obtain a valid
+> oracle call: four consecutive redraws returned OpenRouter HTTP 403 “Key limit
+> exceeded.” The harness-owned error transcript is preserved. This directory is not
+> ready to submit until the bare, structural-hint, and placebo runs complete.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | graph (with GF(2) certificate recovery) |
+| Certificate | degree-one polynomial over GF(2) |
+| Intuition | invariant: the global label XOR is the cut normal |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | Section 4, proof of Theorem 1 |
+
+## Problem and trust basis
+
+The source paper is de Figueiredo, de Melo, Oliveira, and Silva,
+[*MaxCut on Permutation Graphs is NP-complete*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13955).
+An instance gives a cubic source graph with bit-vector labels and the paper’s exact
+macro description of the two permutations defining its much larger permutation
+graph. The solver returns the coefficients of a linear polynomial over GF(2). Its
+values on the source labels determine a source cut and, through the paper’s map
+`f`, every side of the expanded permutation-graph cut. The checker performs exact
+GF(2) dot products and source-edge counting. It never reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse. It samples the polynomial first, builds a connected simple
+cubic bipartite graph, samples vertex labels in the two required half-spaces, and
+conditions the label XOR to equal the polynomial. Full rank of the edge-difference
+vectors makes the bounded answer unique. Section 4’s exact inequality then carries
+the all-edge source cut to a cut meeting the displayed permutation-graph threshold.
+
+This is not Track A. Theorem 1 proves worst-case NP-completeness by reduction from
+cubic MaxCut; it says nothing about the distribution generated here. Section 5 also
+notes a polynomial algorithm on the threshold-graph subclass. For this distribution,
+dense Gauss–Jordan elimination on
+`<c,label[u] XOR label[v]> = 1` is a disclosed `O(m d^2)` algorithm. At the hard
+preset it solved 8/8, took a median 815,912 counted bit operations and about 0.010–
+0.015 seconds locally. The compact route is 115 word XORs plus 96 bit extractions,
+211 exact operations total. The intended difficulty is noticing that compression,
+not denying the efficient algorithm.
+
+The macro form is necessary and paper-licensed: a cubic source on `N` vertices
+expands to `122N^3 + 102N^2 + 11N` graph vertices. Even `N=6` gives 30,090; the
+shipping preset denotes 191,803,100 vertices. The renderer retains the paper’s own
+block permutations rather than replacing them with an unrelated graph surrogate.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders the following instance data (the constant
+block definition and answer contract immediately following it are reproduced too):
+
+```text
+Find a compressed cut certificate for a permutation graph.
+
+All bit arithmetic below is over GF(2). Bit position 0 is the least significant
+bit. For c=(c_0,...,c_7), P_c(x)=sum(c_t*x_t) modulo 2.
+
+Labels:
+  v0: 0x67  v1: 0x6c  v2: 0xd5  v3: 0x7f
+  v4: 0xa2  v5: 0xab  v6: 0x33  v7: 0x8a
+  v8: 0x9c  v9: 0xee  v10: 0x02 v11: 0xf1
+
+Edges:
+  e0=(v2,v3)   e1=(v6,v10)  e2=(v7,v11)  e3=(v5,v8)
+  e4=(v0,v4)   e5=(v5,v10)  e6=(v0,v5)   e7=(v9,v11)
+  e8=(v1,v6)   e9=(v2,v7)   e10=(v6,v11) e11=(v8,v9)
+  e12=(v0,v3)  e13=(v9,v10) e14=(v2,v4)  e15=(v3,v8)
+  e16=(v1,v7)  e17=(v1,v4)
+
+For every source vertex vi create K'_i,K''_i of size q and S'_i,S''_i of
+size p. For every edge ej create K'^e_j,K''^e_j of size q_e and
+S'^e_j,S''^e_j of size p_e. For every incidence (vi,ej), create link vertices
+L1_(i,j),L2_(i,j), and let C_i list its six links in increasing j. With every
+block in a fixed internal order, define
+
+  pi1_i    = K'_i S'_i S''_i C_i K''_i
+  pi2_i    = S'_i rev(K''_i) rev(K'_i) S''_i
+  gamma2_j = S'^e_j rev(K''^e_j) rev(K'^e_j) S''^e_j
+  gamma1_j = K'^e_j L2_(i',j) L1_(i',j) S'^e_j
+             L2_(i,j) L1_(i,j) S''^e_j K''^e_j,
+
+where ej=(vi,vi') and i<i'. The permutations are
+Pi=pi1_0...pi1_11 gamma2_0...gamma2_17 and
+Pi'=pi2_0...pi2_11 gamma1_0...gamma1_17. Two vertices are adjacent exactly
+when their relative order is reversed. Here p=3960, q=1873, p_e=1656,
+q_e=721, and the target is T=276432762.
+
+The polynomial splits source vertices by P_c(label). The induced expanded cut
+uses the paper's map f: K'_i,S''_i and vi's links follow vi; K''_i,S'_i are
+opposite; each edge gadget follows its lower-index endpoint in the analogous
+K'^e,S''^e versus K''^e,S'^e split. The cut reaches T iff all 18 source edges
+cross.
+
+Give exactly eight bits c_0...c_7 inside the tags:
+<answer>00001001</answer>
+```
+
+`verify(inst, [0,0,0,0,1,0,0,1])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Flipping `c_0`
+returns `(False, "source edge e1=(v6,v10) is not crossed")`. A person can solve
+the demo by XORing the twelve two-digit hexadecimal labels and writing the eight
+least-significant-bit-first coefficients.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | `d` | source vertices | source edges | expanded vertices | compact operations | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 12 | 18 | 225,636 | 19 | hand-scale |
+| easy | 40 | 60 | 90 | 26,719,860 | 99 | oracle run attempted, no valid call |
+| medium | 64 | 84 | 126 | 73,030,524 | 147 | locally checked |
+| hard | 96 | 116 | 174 | 191,803,100 | 211 | current shipping candidate |
+
+Escalation keeps the 96-bit answer fixed and increases the source order to 156
+and then 196, growing the equation haystack rather than the witness.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 planted verifies | 16/16 preset/seed checks |
+| G2 corruption | 5/5 rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 round trip | tagged model-style response recovered exactly |
+| G4 random guess | 0/200,000; language size `2^96` |
+| G5 density/cost | exact density `1/2^96`; 4,096-restart attack ≈0.93 s |
+| G6 adversaries | four attacks 0/8 each; reference elimination 8/8 |
+| G7 scaling | `d=192,N=232` built and verified; 1,528,927,096 expanded vertices |
+| G8 canonical key | 20/20 relabellings invariant and witness-preserving; 20/20 distinct |
+| G9(c) caps | 193 JSON chars, 49 estimated tokens, 96 atoms, 211 operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostic
+
+The required bare run produced no counted attempts. The harness redrew four times,
+all ending in the same account-level HTTP 403, then stopped as designed.
+
+| Arm | Solved / valid attempts | Status |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | blocked by OpenRouter key limit |
+| structural | 0 / 0 | not run |
+| placebo | 0 / 0 | not run |
+
+`hinted − placebo` is therefore not yet measurable, and no conclusion about model
+response to the invariant is claimed. The G9 size/effort cap itself passes.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2202_13955 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+prompt = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + "".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit after the oracle evidence is complete with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2202.13955 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is a succinct, paper-licensed reduction instance, not an explicitly listed
+  191-million-vertex adjacency structure. Exact verification uses Section 4’s block
+  identity; it does not enumerate every expanded edge.
+- The source graph is deliberately bipartite and easy to 2-color. That produces a
+  vertex partition, but the required bounded witness is the unique linear normal;
+  recovering it mechanically still needs the disclosed GF(2) solve.
+- `P(random guess)=1/2^d` is exact because the edge-difference system has full rank.
+  It describes a uniform prior on shape-correct coefficient vectors, not a solver
+  exploiting the global XOR invariant.
+- No SDP, spectral relaxation, or general exact MaxCut implementation was run on the
+  expanded graph. The specialized elimination reference is strictly more relevant
+  to the certificate language, but this remains an untested attack surface.
+- Canonicalization covers source-vertex renaming, edge reordering, and endpoint
+  reversal. It does not quotient by arbitrary `GL(d,2)` changes of label basis.
+- Most importantly, the multi-vendor hardening and both extra G9 arms remain blocked
+  by the external account limit. Local gates alone are not evidence that models fail.
