    17	## Problem and trust basis
    18	
    19	The source is de Figueiredo, de Melo, Oliveira, and Silva,
    20	[*MaxCut on Permutation Graphs is NP-complete*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13955).
    21	An instance gives a labeled cubic graph and the paper’s exact block-macro form of
    22	the two permutations defining the much larger permutation graph. The solver returns
    23	a GF(2) linear polynomial. Its values on the source labels define a source cut and,
    24	through the paper’s map `f`, every side of the expanded cut. Verification performs
    25	exact dot products and checks every source edge; it never reads the planted answer.
    26	
    27	Generation is inverse. A dense even-weight polynomial is sampled first. A connected
    28	simple cubic bipartite graph with exactly one four-cycle is sampled next. Labels are
    29	drawn from the polynomial’s two parity half-spaces, with the four cycle labels
    30	conditioned to XOR to the polynomial. Full rank of all edge-difference vectors makes
    31	the bounded certificate unique. Since all source edges cross, Section 4’s inequality
    32	certifies that the mapped permutation-graph cut reaches the threshold. No MaxCut or
    33	linear system is solved during generation.
