    10	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    11	| Native domain / regime | combinatorics / finite field |
    12	| Core / certificate | graph / degree-one polynomial over GF(2) |
    13	| Intuition | invariant: the unique four-cycle’s label XOR is the cut normal |
    14	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    15	| Reduction | Section 4, proof of Theorem 1 |
    16	
    17	## Problem and trust basis
    18	
    19	The source is de Figueiredo, de Melo, Oliveira, and Silva,
    20	[*MaxCut on Permutation Graphs is NP-complete*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13955).
    21	An instance gives a labeled cubic graph and the paper’s exact block-macro form of
    22	the two permutations defining the much larger permutation graph. The solver returns
    23	a GF(2) linear polynomial. Its values on the source labels define a source cut and,
    24	through the paper’s map `f`, every side of the expanded cut. Verification performs
    25	exact dot products, checks every source edge, counts link-link inversions, and
    26	evaluates the paper's weighted block formula; it never reads the planted answer.
    27	
    28	Generation is inverse. A dense even-weight polynomial is sampled first. A connected
    29	simple cubic bipartite graph with exactly one four-cycle is sampled next. Labels are
    30	drawn from the polynomial’s two parity half-spaces, with the four cycle labels
    31	conditioned to XOR to the polynomial. Full rank of all edge-difference vectors makes
    32	the bounded certificate unique. Since all source edges cross, Section 4’s inequality
    33	certifies that the mapped permutation-graph cut reaches the threshold. No MaxCut or
    34	linear system is solved during generation.
    35	
    36	This is deliberately not a Track A claim. Theorem 1 is worst-case NP-completeness
    37	from cubic MaxCut and does not make this generated distribution hard; Section 5 also
    38	identifies threshold graphs as an easy subclass. Here dense Gauss–Jordan elimination
    39	on `<c,label[u] XOR label[v]> = 1` is a disclosed `O(m d^2)` algorithm. On eight hard
    40	instances it solved 8/8 with a median 27,297 counted bit operations and 0.000240 s.
    41	The compact route finds the unique four-cycle and XORs its four labels. Counting an
    42	edge insertion, a cubic wedge inspection, every XORed bit, and every emitted bit, it
    43	uses 281 elementary operations. The benchmark asks whether a solver notices this
    44	roughly 99-fold compression.
    45	
    46	The macro representation is paper-licensed and lossless. A source on `N` vertices
    47	expands to `122N^3 + 102N^2 + 11N` vertices, so explicitly listing the shipping graph
    48	would obscure rather than test the reduction. As a separate construction audit, the
    49	macros were expanded for `N=16` into two genuine 526,000-element permutations; exact
    50	inversion counting obtained 1,120,271,512 crossing edges against threshold
    51	1,120,270,296, with the link correction inside Section 4's bound.
    52	
    53	## Worked demo
    54	
    55	For `demo`, seed 0, the source data are:
    56	
    57	```text
    58	labels: v0=df v1=e5 v2=6f v3=1a v4=4e v5=72 v6=43 v7=9a
    59	        v8=91 v9=68 v10=30 v11=48 v12=84 v13=17 v14=e3 v15=5d
    60	edges:  (1,13) (8,15) (3,11) (9,13) (10,12) (0,10) (0,11) (4,9)
    61	        (4,6) (1,2) (5,8) (3,7) (5,9) (12,15) (3,5) (12,13)
    62	        (6,10) (14,15) (6,7) (4,14) (1,7) (0,2) (8,11) (2,14)
    63	```
    64	
    65	The only four-cycle has vertices `(3,5,8,11)`. Its labels XOR to `0xb1`, whose
    66	least-significant-bit-first coefficients are `<answer>10001101</answer>`.
    67	`verify(inst, [1,0,0,0,1,1,0,1])` returns `(True, "ok")`; flipping coefficient 0
    68	returns `(False, "source edge e3=(v9,v13) is not crossed")`. This demo is genuinely
    69	hand-solvable: find four edges forming the cycle and do three two-hex-digit XORs.
    70	
    71	## Difficulty and gates
    72	
    73	| Preset | d | source V / E | expanded V | compact ops | Status |
    74	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    75	| demo | 8 | 16 / 24 | 526,000 | 104 | hand example |
    76	| easy | 20 | 24 / 36 | 1,745,544 | 188 | first bare oracle rung; API blocked |
    77	| medium | 26 | 28 / 42 | 2,758,420 | 230 | locally verified |
    78	| **hard** | **32** | **34 / 51** | **4,913,374** | **281** | shipping candidate |
    79	
    80	`escalate()` keeps the 32-bit answer fixed and raises the source order through 36
    81	and 38. Beyond that the explicitly counted compact route exceeds the 300-operation
    82	cap, so it returns `"cap_bound"` rather than misclassifying the paper as exhausted.
    83	
    84	| Gate | Measured result |
    85	|---|---|
    86	| G1 | 16/16 planted and four-cycle-invariant checks passed |
    87	| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
    88	| G3 | model-style tagged response round-tripped; garbage returned `None` |
    89	| G4 | 0/200,000 uniform 32-bit guesses; exact language size `2^32` |
    90	| G5 | exact density `1/2^32`; 4,096 restarts took 0.0413 s |
    91	| G6 | four attacks 0/8 each; reference elimination 8/8 |
    92	| G7 | `d=64,N=68` built and verified; 38,833,100 expanded vertices |
    93	| G8 | 100/100 individual/composed relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    94	| G9(c) | 65 JSON chars, 17 estimated tokens, 32 atoms, 281 operations |
    95	
    96	## Oracle loop
    97	
    98	No oracle result may be counted as a failure: every call failed before inference.
    99	
   100	| Call | Preset | Seed | Model | Solved | Why |
   101	|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|
   102	| 1 | easy | 215313271 | `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
   103	| 2 | easy | 652392299 | `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
   104	| 3 | easy | 416356736 | `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
   105	| 4 | easy | 1277773033 | `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
   106	
   107	## G9 diagnostic
   108	
   109	| Arm | Preset | Valid solved / attempts | Script result |
   110	|---|---|---:|---|
   111	| bare | hard | 0 / 0 | four HTTP 403 redraws |
   112	| structural | hard | 0 / 0 | four HTTP 403 redraws |
   113	| placebo | hard | 0 / 0 | four HTTP 403 redraws |
   114	
   115	`hinted − placebo` is therefore undefined in substance (stored as `0.0` only because
   116	both denominators are zero). No conclusion about the claimed invariant is drawn.
   117	
   118	## Use
   119	
   120	```python
   121	import gen_2202_13955 as g
   122	
   123	inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   124	prompt = g.render(inst)
   125	text = "<answer>" + "".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>"
   126	assert g.verify(inst, g.parse_answer(text)) == (True, "ok")
   127	```
   128	
   129	After valid oracle evidence exists, emit from the repository root with:
   130	
   131	```bash
   132	bash scripts/emit.sh 2202.13955 20
   133	```
   134	
   135	## Caveats
   136	
   137	- The GF(2) labels and polynomial certificate are a benchmark encoding added around
   138	  the paper’s Section 4 reduction; they are not objects introduced by the authors.
   139	  The actual permutation graph and cut map remain present and exact, but the search
   140	  is carried by the labeled cubic source, as `licensed_reduction` declares.
   141	- `P(random guess)=1/2^32` is exact only for a uniform prior over shape-correct
   142	  coefficient vectors. It says nothing about a solver that notices the four-cycle.
   143	- The source is bipartite, so a BFS can recover its vertex cut cheaply. Converting
   144	  that cut to the required polynomial still needs the disclosed full-rank solve.
   145	- No SDP, spectral relaxation, or general exact MaxCut implementation was run on the
   146	  4.9-million-vertex expansion. The specialized exact elimination is the relevant
   147	  successful reference algorithm, but those remain untested attack surfaces.
   148	- Canonicalization quotients identifier renaming only when the paper’s vertex and
   149	  edge block orders are carried with it, and also quotients every common invertible
   150	  affine change of GF(2) label coordinates; it correctly retains the structural orders.
   151	- Most importantly, local gates are not multi-vendor hardness evidence. Submission
   152	  remains blocked until OpenRouter permits three valid attempts in each required run.

